<DocsLayout title="How Memory Matures" description="The densification story: from 23 nodes and 1 link on day 1 to a 71-node reasoning web in 26 days — and why graph density is what turns retrieval into advice.">
  <h1>How Memory Matures</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Engrams does not ship smart — it <em>becomes</em> smart, on your project, at the speed you
    log to it. The data below is not illustrative. It is this repository's own knowledge graph,
    measured at three points over its first 26 days of real feature work.
  </p>

  <div class="grid grid-4 stat-row reveal-stagger">
    <div class="card stat">
      <div class="stat-num">23<span class="stat-sub">/1</span></div>
      <div class="stat-label">Day 1 · nodes/links</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <div class="stat-num">43<span class="stat-sub">/11</span></div>
      <div class="stat-label">Week 1 · nodes/links</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <div class="stat-num">71<span class="stat-sub">/29</span></div>
      <div class="stat-label">Month 1 · nodes/links</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card stat">
      <div class="stat-num">142<span class="stat-sub">/62</span></div>
      <div class="stat-label">v0.12.0 · nodes/links</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="reveal">
    <h2>The evolution of advice</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Graph</th><th>What engrams delivers</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Day 1</strong></td>
          <td>23 nodes, 1 link</td>
          <td>
            <strong>Immediate Token Savings.</strong> Decisions capture exact interface contracts via <code>--contract</code>. Even with a single link, the AI avoids opening 300-line source files on prompt #1.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Week 1</strong></td>
          <td>43 nodes, 11 links</td>
          <td>
            <strong>Topological Linking.</strong> The first reasoning links appear: <code>supersedes</code> and <code>extends</code>. Code nodes extract exported symbols and line counts automatically. <code>engrams relevant</code> surfaces anchored constraints before edits.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Month 1</strong></td>
          <td>71 nodes, 29 links</td>
          <td>
            <strong>A Reasoning Web.</strong> Policy engine patterns, contradiction gates, and causal chains (<code>graph why</code>). The AI understands <em>why</em> architecture choices were made and what breaks if a dependency changes.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>v0.12.0</strong></td>
          <td>142 nodes, 62 links, 1,273 edges</td>
          <td>
            <strong>Self-Sufficient Architecture Graph.</strong> Zero-file-read strategy formation via <code>brief</code>, cross-convention FTS, Git staleness drift verification, automated PR validation gates, and measured token ROI tracking.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="reveal">
    <h2>Why density is what makes it advice</h2>
    <p>
      Retrieval quality scales with records. <em>Advice</em> quality scales with connections:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>More anchors for priming.</strong> Every linked decision and pattern is surface
        area for <code>engrams prime</code> and <code>engrams relevant</code> to catch on. A file
        you're about to edit is no longer isolated — it inherits the reasoning of everything
        anchored to it and everything those nodes link to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>More contradictions to catch.</strong> A <code>supersedes</code> link is only
        possible when both decisions exist. Density is what lets <code>engrams check</code> and
        the ontology's disjointness rules detect that your new direction conflicts with a settled
        one — while it is still a staged diff, not a regression.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Longer chains of why.</strong> Month 1's web means a question like “why does the
        policy engine write through to the workspace DB?” resolves through
        <code>part_of</code> → #44 → <code>refines</code> → the omp-integration decision. Sparse
        graphs answer questions; dense graphs answer <em>follow-up</em> questions.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout tip">
      <strong>The multiplier</strong>
      The 29 manual reasoning links are only the seed. Engrams materializes derived edges from
      them — inverses, transitive closures, git co-change topology. The full graph behind these
      71 knowledge nodes carries <strong>802 edges</strong>: 45 manual, 757 derived, across 67
      code nodes. Every link you add is amplified.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="reveal">
    <h2>The arc is the point</h2>
    <p>
      None of this required curation sessions or documentation sprints. The agent logged
      decisions as they were made and linked them as relationships became clear — and 26 days
      later the graph had crossed from storage into advice. The cycle that produces this density
      is short enough to run on every task:
      <a href="/docs/advisor/loop">The Advisor Loop</a>. To see the graph itself, go back to
      <a href="/docs/advisor/overview">What is an Advisor?</a>
    </p>
  </div>
